                  %a{:href => "#DecalageComonad"} Décalage comonad
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#total_dcalage"} Total Décalage
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ExampleForSimplicialClassifyingSpaces"} For simplicial classifying spaces
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ExampleForSimplicialGroups"} For simplicial groups
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+theory"} simplicial homotopy theory
            %em décalage
            refers to a well-behaved model of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space+objects"} path space objects
            for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %p
            If you take a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            and ‘throw away’ the last face and degeneracy, and relabel, shifting everything down one ‘notch’, you get a new simplicial set. This is what is called the
            %em décalage
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
          %p
            It is a model for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space+object"} path space object
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X_0
            – similar to, but a little smaller than, the model
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^I \times_X X_0
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+lemma"} factorization lemma
          %p
            In the latter case an
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            from the simplicial cone over the
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Delta[n] \times \Delta[1]) \coprod_{\Delta[n]} \Delta[0]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder"} cylinder
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n]
            and then contracting one end to the point.
          %p
            Contrary to that, an
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a morphism to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            from the cone over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n]
            modeled simply by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join+of+simplicial+sets"} join of simplicial sets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n] \star \Delta[0]
          %p
            This is a much smaller model for the cone. In fact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n]\star \Delta[0] = \Delta[n+1]
            is just the
            = succeed "-simplex." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-simplices," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ↪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta[n] \star \Delta[1] \hookrightarrow
                  (\Delta[n] \times \Delta[1]) \coprod_{\Delta[n]} \Delta[0]
                  \\,.
          %p
            As a result, the décalage construction is often more convenient than forming
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^I \times_X X_0
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p The plain definition of the décalage of a simplicial set is very simple, stated below in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#DefinitionInComponents"} In components
          %p However, in order to appreciate and handle this definition, it is useful to understand it as a special case of total décalage, stated below in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#AsARestrictionOfTotalDecalage"} As a restriction of total décalage
              \.
          %p From this one sees more manifestly that the décalage of a simplicial set is built from cones in the original simplicial set. This we discuss below in
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#DefinitionInTermsOfCones"} In terms of cones
              \.
          %p In this last formulation it is clearest what the two canonical morphisms out of the décalage of a simplicial set mean. These we define in
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#MorphismsOutOfIt"} Morphisms out of décalage
              \.
          %h3#DefinitionInComponents In components
          %p Concretely, the décalage construction is the following.
          #DecalageInComponents.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              %strong décalage
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0\, X \in sSet
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              in each dimension:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi Dec
                          %mn 0
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi X
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi n
                        %mo ≔
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Dec_0 \, X)_n \coloneqq X_{n+1}
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi d
                          %mi k
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %msub
                              %mi Dec
                              %mn 0
                            %mi X
                        %mo ≔
                        %msubsup
                          %mi d
                          %mi k
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_k^{n,Dec_0 X}  \coloneqq d^{n+1,X}_{k}
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi s
                          %mi k
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %msub
                              %mi Dec
                              %mn 0
                            %mi X
                        %mo ≔
                        %msubsup
                          %mi s
                          %mi k
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_k^{n,Dec_0 X}  \coloneqq s^{n+1,X}_{k}
                  \.
          %p Analogously:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi Dec
                        %mn 0
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi n
                      %mo ≔
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Dec^0 \, X)_n \coloneqq X_{n+1}
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi d
                        %mi k
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ,
                          %msup
                            %mi Dec
                            %mn 0
                          %mi X
                      %mo ≔
                      %msubsup
                        %mi d
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_k^{n,Dec^0 X}  \coloneqq d^{n+1,X}_{k+1}
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi s
                        %mi k
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ,
                          %msup
                            %mi Dec
                            %mn 0
                          %mi X
                      %mo ≔
                      %msubsup
                        %mi s
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_k^{n,Dec^0 X}  \coloneqq s^{n+1,X}_{k+1}
                \.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Illusie72"}> Illusie 72
            , review in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Stevenson11"} Stevenson 11, Def. 2
          %h3#AsARestrictionOfTotalDecalage As a restriction of total décalage
          %p
            It is often useful to understand this as a special case of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+d%C3%A9calage"} total décalage
            construction:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi a
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \colon \Delta_a \times \Delta_a \to \Delta_a
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+sum"} ordinal sum
              operation on the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asSet"} augmented simplex category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+d%C3%A9calage"} total décalage
              functor is precompositon with this
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi ssSet
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\sigma^* \colon sSet_a \to ssSet_a
            %p
              or rather its restriction from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/augmented+simplicial+sets"} augmented simplicial sets
              to just
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimplicial+sets"} bisimplicial sets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi sSet
                    %mo →
                    %mi ssSet
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\sigma^* : sSet \to ssSet
                    \\,.
            %p
              In terms of this the plain
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/d%C3%A9calage"} décalage
              is the functor induced from the restriction
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo →
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma(-,[0]) : \Delta \to \Delta
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+sum"} ordinal sum
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Dec_0 X := (\sigma(-,[0]))^* X
                    \\,.
          %h3#DefinitionInTermsOfCones In terms of cones
          %p
            The perspective from total décalage makes fairly manifest that décalage forms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cones"} cones
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+d%C3%A9calage"} total décalage
            to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join+of+simplicial+sets"} join of simplicial sets
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                    %mo :
                    %mi sSet
                    %mo ×
                    %mi sSet
                    %mo →
                    %mi ssSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Box : sSet \times sSet \to ssSet
            %p
              for the
              %em box product functor
              that takes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y \in sSet
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimplicial+set"} bisimplicial set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo □
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi k
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mi l
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (X \Box Y) : ([k],[l]) \mapsto X_k \times Y_l
                    \\,.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
              are connected, then their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join+of+simplicial+sets"} join of simplicial sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \star Y
              is expressed by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+d%C3%A9calage"} total décalage
              as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo □
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\sigma_!(X \Box Y) = X \star Y
                    \\,.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Stevenson12"}> Stevenson 12, lemma 2.1
            ).
          %p It follows that the left adjoint of plain décalage forms joins with the 0-simplex:
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %mi sSet
                    %mo →
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0 : sSet \to sSet
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo □
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C := \sigma_!((-) \Box \Delta[0])
                    \\,.
            %p
              In particular for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \in sSet
              connected we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C(S) = S \star \Delta[0]
                    \\,.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Stevenson12"}> Stevenson 12, cor. 2.1
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join+of+simplicial+sets"} join of simplicial sets
              with the 0-simplex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \star \Delta[0]
              forms a simplicial model for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          #InTermsOfCones.num_cor
            %h6#corollary_2 Corollary
            %p
              By
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              we have for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi sSet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (Dec_0 X)_n = Hom_{sSet}( \Delta[n] \star \Delta[0], X)
                    \\,.
          %p
            So this exhibits the
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Dec
                    %mn 0
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0 X
            as being the cones of
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h3#MorphismsOutOfIt Morphisms out of the décalage
          #TheCanonicalMorphisms.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in sSet
              its décalage
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0 X
              comes with two canonical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              out of it
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Dec
                              %mn 0
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi Fib
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} W
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi const
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    Dec_0 X &amp;\overset{\in Fib}{\longrightarrow}&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow {}^{\mathrlap{\in \mathrm{W}}}
                    \\\
                    const X_0
                    \\mathrlap{\,.}
                    }
            %p
              Here, in terms of the description
              %a{:href => "#DefinitionInTermsOfCones"} above
              of décalage by cones:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the horizontal morphism is induced from the canonical inclusion
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo ↪
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo ⋆
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n] \hookrightarrow \Delta[n]\star \Delta[0]
              %li
                %p
                  the vertical morphism is given by the canonical inclusion
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo ↪
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo ⋆
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[0] \hookrightarrow \Delta[n]\star \Delta[0]
            %p
              Or in terms of components, as discussed
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#DefinitionInComponents"} above
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the horizontal morphism is given by
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mi last
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %msub
                            %mi Dec
                            %mn 0
                          %mi Y
                          %mo →
                          %mi Y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{last} \colon Dec_0 Y \to Y
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  by the remaining face map
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{n+1} \colon X_{n+1} \to X_n
              %li
                %p
                  the vertical morphism is given in degree 0 by
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_0 \colon X_1 \to X_0
                  and in every higher degree similarly by
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi s
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ∘
                          %msub
                            %mi s
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo ∘
                          %msub
                            %mi s
                            %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_0 \circ s_0 \circ \cdots \circ s_0
          %p
            (review in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Stevenson11"} Stevenson 11, Def. 2
          %p
            These morphisms are a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
            and a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalence"} simplicial weak equivalence
            %a{:href => "#FibrationResolution"} following
            discussion.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#FibrationResolution Fibration resolution
          %p
            We discuss here how
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Dec
                    %mn 0
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0 X \to X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi const
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} const X_0 \to X
            by a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a simplicial set, the two morphisms from prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheCanonicalMorphisms"}
              have the following properties.
            %ul
              %li
                the morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Dec
                        %mn 0
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi const
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0 : Dec_0 X \to const X_0
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+retract"} deformation retract
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mn 0
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_0 : X_0 \to X_1
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            %ul
              %li
                the morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi last
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Dec
                        %mn 0
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{last} : Dec_0 X \to X
                is a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The first statement is classical, it appears for instance as (
              %a{:href => "#Stevenson11"}> Stevenson 11, lemma 5
              ).
            %p
              For the second, notice that by remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InTermsOfCones"}
              the lifting problem
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi Λ
                              %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Dec
                              %mn 0
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Lambda^n[n] &amp;\to&amp; Dec_0 X
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\Delta[n] &amp;\to&amp; X
                    }
            %p is equivalent to the lifting problem
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi Λ
                              %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ⋆
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi Λ
                                  %mi i
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                %mi n
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ⋆
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    (\Lambda^n[n] \star \Delta[0]) \coprod_{\Lambda^i[n]} \Delta[n] &amp;\to&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\Delta[n] \star \Delta[0] &amp;\to&amp; *
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Here the left morphism is an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anodyne+morphism"} anodyne morphism
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
              inclusion
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Λ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda[n+1] \to \Delta[n+1]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              By the above,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0 X
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+quasi-categories"} over quasi-categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Dec_0 X  = \coprod_{x \in X_0} X_{/x}
                    \\,.
            %p
              For each of these the statement that the projection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{/x} \to X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+fibration"} right fibration
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "#Joyal"} Joyal, theorem 3.19
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop. 2.1.2.1
              = succeed ")." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "right%2Fleft+Kan+fibration#AsFibrationsInInfinityGroupoids"} Left fibration in ∞-groupoids
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary_3 Corollary
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0 X \to X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
              of the inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi const
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} const X_0 \to X
              of the set of 0-cells of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object"} discrete
              simplicial set:
            %p
              there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi const
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Dec
                              %mn 0
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    const X_0 &amp;\stackrel{\simeq}{\to}&amp; Dec_0 X
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    X &amp;\to&amp; X
                    }
                    \\,,
            %p where
            %ul
              %li
                %p the top morphism
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      is given in degree
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                      by the
                      = succeed "-fold" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi s
                                %mn 0
                              %mo ∘
                              %msub
                                %mi s
                                %mn 0
                              %mo ∘
                              %mi ⋯
                              %msub
                                %mi s
                                %mn 0
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_0 \circ s_0 \circ \cdots s_0
                  %li
                    %p
                      is a
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
              %li
                %p the right vertical morphism
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      is given in degree
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                      by
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mrow
                                %mi n
                                %mo +
                                %mn 1
                            %mo :
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mrow
                                %mi n
                                %mo +
                                %mn 1
                            %mo →
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mi n
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{n+1} : X_{n+1} \to X_n
                  %li
                    %p
                      is a
                      = succeed "." do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              The inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi const
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} const X_0 \to X
              presents a canonical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphism+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} effective epimorphism in an (∞,1)-category
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
              into
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated"} 0-truncated
              object. By the above, the décalage is a natural fibration resolution of this canonical “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"}> atlas
              ”.
            %p
              This is useful for instance in the discussion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullbacks"} homotopy pullbacks
              of this effective epimorphism: by the discussion there the homotopy pullback of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi const
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} const X_0 \to X
              along any morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to X
              is presented by the ordinary pullback of any Kan fibration resolution, hence in particular of the décalage projection:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi A
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mi h
                    %mi const
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f^* Dec_0 X \simeq A \times_X^{h} const X_0
                    \\,.
          %h3#DecalageComonad Décalage comonad
          %p
            Décalage also has an abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretic
            description as follows. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/augmented+simplex+category"} augmented simplex category
            with the ordinal sum operation is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mo =
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Delta_a, + = \sigma, 0 = [-1])
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 = [0]
            with its unique monoid structure. By duality, the monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a^{op}
            has a comonoid, also denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
          %p
            As is the case for any comonoid in a monoidal category, the comonoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
            induces a comonad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_0 = (-) + [0] = \sigma(-, [0])
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi a
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a^{op}
            = succeed "-functor," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{-}
            as a
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi cat
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^{op} \to Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_0: \Delta_a^{op} \to \Delta_a^{op}
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi cat
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^{op}
            to a comonad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi a
                        %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi a
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{D_0}: Set^{\Delta_a^{op}} \to Set^{\Delta_a^{op}}
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: \Delta_a^{op} \to Set
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi a
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \circ D_0: \Delta_a^{op} \to Set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %mi SSet
                    %mo →
                    %mi SSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0: SSet \to SSet
          %p
            (By similar reasoning, there is a second comonad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^0 = [0] + (-) = \sigma([0], -)
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi a
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a^{op}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi a
                          %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi a
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{D^0}: Set^{\Delta_a^{op}} \to Set^{\Delta_a^{op}}
            %a{:href => "#Stevenson11"} Stevenson
            as
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %mi SSet
                    %mo →
                    %mi SSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec^0: SSet \to SSet
          %p
            There are tautologically equivalent formulations. One formulation invokes the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a
            together with the terminal monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
            constitute the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+structure"} walking
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a
            is initial among monoidal categories equipped with a monoid. Similarly,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a^{op}
            is the walking
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonoid"} comonoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a^{op}\to [C, C]
            are precisely in correspondence with comonoids in the endofunctor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C, C]
            (as a monoidal category under endofunctor composition), that is to say, with comonads on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p Consider then the monoidal product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : \Delta_a^{op} \times \Delta_a^{op} \to \Delta_a^{op}
          %p
            Analogous to Cayley embeddings of monoids into endofunction monoids, either way of currying this product produces a strict monoidal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a^{op}\to [\Delta_a^{op},\Delta_a^op]
            into an endofunctor category. By applying 2-functoriality as above, there is additionally a strict monoidal functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi SSet
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi SSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta_a^{op}, \Delta_a^{op}] \to [\mathbf{SSet},\mathbf{SSet}]
          %p given by precomposition. Composing these two strict monoidal functors, there is a strict monoidal functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi SSet
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi SSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a^{op}\to [\mathbf{SSet}, \mathbf{SSet}].
          %p
            Hence by the “walking” correspondence, the value of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
            under this monoidal functor is a comonad on simplicial sets whose underlying functor is décalage:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Dec
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi a
                        %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi a
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Dec_0 \colon Set^{\Delta_a^{op}} \to Set^{\Delta_a^{op}}
          %p (Tautologically, though, this is merely an elaborate way to rephrase the earlier description of this comonad.)
          %p
            The map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi last
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi Dec
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi Id
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{last} \colon Dec_0 \to Id
            is the counit of the comonad. The comonad itself is analogous to a kind of unbased
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space+object"} path space object
            comonad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            whose value at a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi I
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi eval
                                %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi i
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  P X &amp; \to &amp; X^I
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{eval_0}
                  \\\
                  |X| &amp; \stackrel{i}{\to} &amp; X
                  }
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is the set-theoretic identity inclusion of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            equipped with the discrete topology. Thus we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P X
                  \\;=\;
                  \\sum_{x_0 \in X} P(X, x_0),
          %p
            the sum over all possible basepoints
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            of path spaces based at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Dec
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi Sing
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≅
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Sing
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi P
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Dec_0 \circ Sing
                  \\;\cong\;
                  Sing \circ P
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sing
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Top
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing \;\colon\; Top \to Set^{\Delta^{op}}
            is the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complex"} singular simplicial complex
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-coalgebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            into path components and exhibits contractibility of each component. Similarly, a coalgebra of the décalage comonad exhibits the acyclicity of the underlying simplicial set.
          %h4#total_dcalage Total Décalage
          %p
            Using either the simplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonadic+resolution"} comonadic resolution
            generated by the above comonad or directly using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+sum"} ordinal sum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimplicial+set"} bisimplicial set
            known as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+decalage"} total décalage
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#ExampleForSimplicialClassifyingSpaces For simplicial classifying spaces
          %p
            A central application is the special case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi W
                    %mo ¯
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \overline{W} G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+classifying+space"} simplicial classifying space
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+principal+bundle"} simplicial principal bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mi W
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0 \overline{W} G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W G
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "simplicial%20principal%20bundle#UniversalSimplicialBundle"} universal simplicial principal bundle
          %p
            Or rather, with the conventions used at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+classifying+spaces"} simplicial classifying spaces
            (which are those of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "simplicial+classifying+space#GoerssJardine09"} Goerss &amp; Jardine, p. 269
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi Dec
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi W
                    %mo ¯
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W G \,=\, Dec^0(\overline{W}G)
            (Def.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DecalageInComponents"}
          %h3#ExampleForSimplicialGroups For simplicial groups
          %p
            The case of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Dec
                    %mn 0
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dec_0 G
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
            is important in the simplicial theory of algebraic models for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-types"} homotopy n-types
          %p
            In this case the morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi last
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Dec
                      %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{last} : Dec_0\, G \to G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            of this and then applying
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+module"} crossed module
            constructed from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi NG
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N G_1/d_2(NG_2)\to N G_0,
          %p
            which has kernel
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(G)
            and cokernel
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(G)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-type"} homotopy 2-type
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+square"} crossed square
            which represents the 3-type of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+construction"} bar construction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+of+a+bisimplicial+set"} diagonal of a bisimplicial set
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+classifying+space"} simplicial classifying space
          %h2#references References
          %p Original sources are
          %ul
            %li#Illusie72
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luc+Illusie"}> Luc Illusie
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Complexe cotangent et déformations I
              = succeed "," do
                %em Complexe cotangent et déformations II
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Duskin"}> John Duskin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Simplicial methods and the interpretation of “triple” cohomology
          %p
            The notion of décalage has been widely used since the paper introducing the method of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomological+descent"} cohomological descent
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Théorie de Hodge. III
              %strong 44
              (1974), 5–77.
          %p Reviews are in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Ehlers"}> Phil Ehlers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic Homotopy in Simplicially Enriched Groupoids
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Ehlers-thesis.pdf", :title => ""} here
          %p A detailed account of various technical aspects:
          %ul
            %li#Stevenson11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Danny+Stevenson"}> Danny Stevenson
              , around Lemma 15 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Décalage and Kan’s simplicial loop group functor
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.0474"} arXiv:1112.0474
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/26/28/26-28abs.html"} tac:26-28
          %p and
          %ul
            %li#Stevenson12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Danny+Stevenson"}> Danny Stevenson
              , Section 2.2 of
              %em Classifying theory for simplicial parametrized groups
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1203.2461"}> arXiv:1203.2461
              )
          %p Closely related technical results are in section 3 of
          %ul
            %li#Joyal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
              ,
              %em The theory of quasi-categories and its applications
              , lectures at CRM Barcelona (2008)
          %p
            The link with simplicial groups and algebraic models of homotopy
            = succeed "-types" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"}> Tim Porter
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em n-types of simplicial groups and crossed n-cubes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"}> Tim Porter
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Menagerie"} The crossed menagerie
          %p
            An application in the theory of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The stack quotient of a groupoid
              %strong 44
              no. 2 (2003), p. 85–104
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CTGDC_2003__44_2_85_0"} numdam
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction+unit"} adjunction unit
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+d%C3%A9calage"} total décalage
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Viktoriya+Ozornova"}> Viktoriya Ozornova
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martina+Rovelli"} Martina Rovelli
              = succeed "," do
                %em The unit of the total décalage adjunction
